Aspiration pneumonia secondary to dysphagia
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Coughing after swallowing, Fever/tachypnoea, Focal dependent crackles, Right lower lobe infiltrate

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-005
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of fever, respiratory distress, and productive cough in a vulnerable patient.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and facility nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden onset of high fever (38.8 C), progressive tachypnoea, and hypoxaemia following an episode of coughing during lunch. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the rapid decompensation and high risk of progressing to respiratory failure and sepsis, mandating immediate clinical evaluation and commencement of antibiotics.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He started a wet, gurgling cough right after eating pureed food, and now he is burning up and struggling to breathe. (Reported by nursing staff)
History of Present Illness: The nursing staff report a 6-hour history of sudden onset persistent wet, gurgling cough that began immediately following oral intake at lunch. The patient has a documented history of advanced neurodegenerative disease with bulbar palsy. Over the past 4 hours, he has developed a high fever, chills, and progressive tachypnoea. The staff noted purulent sputum production and distinct halitosis. There has been an unintentional weight loss of 3kg over the past month due to recurrent feeding difficulties.
Functional Status: Bedbound; requires total assistance for all activities of daily living and feeding.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent aspiration and rapid clinical deterioration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 38.6 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 89% (Room Air) | Wt: 58.0 Kg
General: Patient is flushed, diaphoretic, and in visible moderate respiratory distress, using accessory muscles.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals focal coarse crackles and bronchial breath sounds localized strictly to the right lower lobe. Dullness to percussion is noted over the right lung base. Halitosis is present, suggesting anaerobic bacterial presence. Cardiovascular exam shows tachycardia with regular rhythm, no murmurs. Neurological exam confirms profound bulbar weakness with an absent gag reflex.
Environmental Observations: Head of the bed was found flat; patient was reportedly fed in a semi-recumbent position.
Medication Reconciliation: Current medications reviewed; patient is not on any regular prophylactic antibiotics or acid-suppressants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Aspiration pneumonia secondary to dysphagia - Status: Exacerbated/Acute
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of high fever, purulent sputum, focal crackles, and the clear temporal relationship between eating and symptom onset.

Congestive Heart Failure Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the unilateral, focal nature of the crackles, absence of jugular venous distension, and absence of peripheral edema.

Viral Upper Respiratory Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely given the severity of hypoxaemia, focal lower lobe findings, and abrupt onset specifically triggered by a swallowing event.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Institutional feeding practices)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of high fever, hypoxaemia, and right lower lobe signs immediately following a choking episode provides clear objective evidence of aspiration pneumonia. The severe derangement in vital signs (SpO2 89%, RR 26) and systemic toxicity indicated rapid clinical deterioration. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because immediate initiation of supplemental oxygen and empirical broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy covering anaerobes was critical to prevent septic shock and respiratory failure, effectively substituting for an emergency department admission within the facility.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced supplemental oxygen at 2L/min via nasal prongs to maintain SpO2 >92%. Administered stat IV Ceftriaxone and oral Metronidazole, followed by charting regular doses. Recommended formal chest radiograph and videofluoroscopic swallowing study when stable.
Positive Behavioral Change: When staff ensure the head of the bed is elevated to 45 degrees during and for 30 minutes after feeds, gravity protects the airway; therefore, implementing a strict positioning checklist builds a culture of safety. When staff perform thorough oral care twice daily, anaerobic bacterial load is reduced; rewarding consistent mouth care compliance reduces future infection severity.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to facility speech pathologist for diet modification review.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure functional suction equipment is kept at the bedside.
Follow-up: Medical review tomorrow morning to assess clinical response to antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 if oxygen saturations drop below 90% despite supplemental oxygen, if systolic blood pressure drops below 90 mmHg, or if the patient becomes unarousable.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on clinical signs; formal imaging is required for definitive confirmation.
Clinical Course: The infection is acute and potentially life-threatening. Response to antibiotics is expected within 48 hours, but the underlying risk of recurrent aspiration remains permanent.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Nursing staff must immediately call for an ambulance if the patient's breathing becomes extremely labored, or if cyanosis of the lips and fingertips occurs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The antibiotics will fight the mixed bacterial infection in the lung caused by inhaled food. The oxygen helps support his breathing while the lungs heal.
Actionable Steps: Maintain strict upright positioning for feeds. Nil by mouth until reviewed by speech pathology. Continue oxygen therapy.

